1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,280
In this video, we're going to show you how you can use Azure DevOps to run a burp CI-driven scan.

2
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:15,100
Now, in our repository here, we have a few files.

3
00:00:15,740 --> 00:00:22,980
We have a configuration file for the pipeline, a configuration file for the scan, a readme file, and a recorded login JSON file.

4
00:00:23,980 --> 00:00:28,140
In our pipeline, all we say is we're going to install Docker on our agent,

5
00:00:28,140 --> 00:00:31,360
and using Docker, we're going to run our enterprise scan container.

6
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:37,300
We're going to bind some directories to the Docker container so we can see our repository code files.

7
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:42,200
We tell the container with an environment variable where it can find the scan configuration file,

8
00:00:42,920 --> 00:00:44,640
where it should output a report file to,

9
00:00:45,480 --> 00:00:51,120
and we also say the URL to the enterprise server and its API key using environment variables

10
00:00:51,120 --> 00:00:54,140
which have been bound to the secrets which have been configured within the pipeline.

11
00:00:54,140 --> 00:00:57,640
We then say that after the scan is completed,

12
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:00,700
we should publish the test results as JUnit report files

13
00:01:00,700 --> 00:01:03,880
based on the ones that we've outputted here,

14
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:05,800
as specified in the environment variable above.

15
00:01:07,380 --> 00:01:11,400
The burp configuration file just says the TLS to our enterprise server,

16
00:01:11,500 --> 00:01:12,760
because we use a self-signed certificate,

17
00:01:12,900 --> 00:01:15,040
we use this to verify the authenticity of the connection.

18
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:18,140
We specify the correlation ID of the site,

19
00:01:18,300 --> 00:01:20,720
which is the name that the site will show us in the enterprise console.

20
00:01:20,720 --> 00:01:23,940
And we also specify all of this configuration here,

21
00:01:24,060 --> 00:01:26,200
which is the same as a normal enterprise configuration

22
00:01:26,200 --> 00:01:28,820
for a scan and a site,

23
00:01:29,240 --> 00:01:30,680
as you can specify through the console.

24
00:01:32,520 --> 00:01:34,960
You can also see the reporting section here,

25
00:01:35,080 --> 00:01:38,140
which specifies that when the scan detects a low severity

26
00:01:38,140 --> 00:01:40,180
with a firm confidence issue,

27
00:01:40,360 --> 00:01:41,260
we're going to fail the build.

28
00:01:41,260 --> 00:01:46,520
The recorded login JSON file is just a standard recorded login JSON

29
00:01:46,520 --> 00:01:49,060
as you paste into the enterprise console.

30
00:01:50,640 --> 00:01:52,060
If we now go to run a pipeline,

31
00:01:54,120 --> 00:01:57,080
and we just kick that off now,

32
00:01:58,540 --> 00:02:02,620
what we can see is the job's going to start,

33
00:02:03,160 --> 00:02:04,500
and it's going to pull down our container,

34
00:02:04,740 --> 00:02:05,960
and we're going to get going with the scan.

35
00:02:06,820 --> 00:02:09,020
And when you see the configuration outputted like this,

36
00:02:09,160 --> 00:02:10,320
you know that the scan has been started.

37
00:02:11,220 --> 00:02:12,880
So if we go to our enterprise console here,

38
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:13,920
and we refresh the page,

39
00:02:14,440 --> 00:02:16,160
you can see here a new scan is starting.

40
00:02:16,920 --> 00:02:18,260
And if I now go and view the site,

41
00:02:18,420 --> 00:02:20,920
you can see that this site was created by ACI-driven scan,

42
00:02:21,060 --> 00:02:23,180
just to prove that this has actually come from Azure DevOps.

43
00:02:24,880 --> 00:02:25,960
Now as the scan progresses,

44
00:02:26,120 --> 00:02:28,140
you can see some different statistics within the pipeline,

45
00:02:28,940 --> 00:02:29,800
showing what's happening,

46
00:02:29,800 --> 00:02:31,640
but rather than waiting for this one to finish,

47
00:02:31,640 --> 00:02:34,500
which is going to go back to our pipelines,

48
00:02:34,700 --> 00:02:36,100
and go to one which we've already run before.

49
00:02:37,220 --> 00:02:38,520
If we go into the job here,

50
00:02:38,640 --> 00:02:39,480
and look at the console,

51
00:02:40,400 --> 00:02:42,020
you can see that as the scan progresses,

52
00:02:42,140 --> 00:02:43,580
you can see your statistics here,

53
00:02:43,720 --> 00:02:44,560
and then it says at the end,

54
00:02:44,640 --> 00:02:45,980
that the issue threshold was breached,

55
00:02:46,500 --> 00:02:48,060
and we're going to exit with a failure code.

56
00:02:49,300 --> 00:02:52,820
Now because we outputted those results as a JUnit XML file,

57
00:02:52,960 --> 00:02:55,360
and we published those right at the end of the pipeline there,

58
00:02:55,740 --> 00:02:57,580
that now means that we can go to the tests,

59
00:02:57,820 --> 00:02:59,520
and see those results directly within the pipeline.

60
00:02:59,520 --> 00:03:00,620
So if I scroll down here,

61
00:03:01,480 --> 00:03:05,960
you can see here that we've got a client-side prototype pollution vulnerability,

62
00:03:06,760 --> 00:03:07,820
which if we scroll down,

63
00:03:08,280 --> 00:03:10,500
you can see the severity and the confidence,

64
00:03:10,820 --> 00:03:12,000
and all of this information here,

65
00:03:12,160 --> 00:03:14,700
which is the same as what you could see within the Enterprise console.

66
00:03:16,060 --> 00:03:18,540
I'm just going to go to this public-facing repository here,

67
00:03:19,160 --> 00:03:21,540
and this shows you all of the configuration,

68
00:03:21,800 --> 00:03:23,220
which you can set for a CI-driven scan.

69
00:03:23,460 --> 00:03:24,760
I only showed a snapshot before,

70
00:03:25,280 --> 00:03:26,780
but if you'd like to look at this in your own time,

71
00:03:26,900 --> 00:03:29,420
you can do it based on the link that I've got on the screen now.

72
00:03:30,480 --> 00:03:32,280
Well, that is how you can run a CI-driven scan

73
00:03:32,280 --> 00:03:34,520
with Bip3 Enterprise and Azure DevOps.

